{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals dusting the Kamo River like confetti. No guidebook could prepare me for the quiet magic of Gion’s wooden machiya houses, their paper lanterns glowing as dusk settled. I sipped matcha in a centuries-old teahouse, the bitterness balanced by a single sweet wagashi, while a geisha’s silk obi whispered past the alleyway.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of chaos and calm. In Shibuya, I stood amid the scramble crossing, overwhelmed yet oddly peaceful. At Shinjuku’s golden alleys, I ate yakitori under neon kanji, laughing with a stranger who taught me “kampai!” with a beer in one hand and a grilled chicken skewer in the other. The next morning, I rode the Shinkansen to Nikko, where torii gates lined forest paths like sentinels of time. At Toshogu Shrine, gold leaf shimmered on cedar carvings—every detail a prayer in wood.\n\nI hiked to a hidden onsen near Hakone, steam curling above the rocks as Mount Fuji rose in the distance, capped in snow even in early spring. No one spoke. We just sat, soaking, watching clouds drift.\n\nIn Osaka, I devoured takoyaki from a street cart, the batter crisp, the octopus tender, the sauce sweet and sticky on my fingers. The vendor grinned, “Oishii?” I nodded, mouth full. He laughed. “That’s the point.”\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—in the rustle of bamboo, the clink of a tea bowl, the bow of a shopkeeper. It’s in the silence between footsteps on temple stone, and the warmth of a stranger’s smile when you’re lost.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of matcha, a faded postcard from Fushimi Inari, and a heart quieter than before.\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I breathed it.\n\n— 298 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob"